@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const anAssertionRestatingTheQueryTheCodeComposesCertifiesAQueryNothingCa
   claim:
     'Seven readout code files compose a query keyed `page-type`, and `asking`\'s `Query` has no such field — it takes `pageTypeSlug`. Four assertions across three test files certify that spelling by reading the same key back off the query the readout itself composed, so the test restates the code rather than judging it. Run against the live index, such a query refuses with "`undefined` names no page type the index holds", and two further faults sit behind it: the page-type name is not a page type, and the keys are kebab where pages carry camel. Nothing typed the seam — `Asking` takes `Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>`, so any object satisfies it. The suite passed 300 of 300 over the `upkeep-surplus` case before it was repaired and passes 255 of 255 at HEAD with the same defect left in `upkeep-capacity`, `upkeep-safety` and `attribute-charisma`.',
   evidence:
-    'Measured 2026-09-05 between 13:20 and 13:50 MDT, read-only. The tree moved under me: HEAD went through four commits while I worked, two of them repairs to this very code.\n\nTHE STATE I FOUND THE NAMED FILE IN. The brief named `readout-system/readouts/pages/upkeep-surplus/upkeep-surplus.readout.test.ts:20`. It had already been repaired. `841c0e961b` of 13:28:58 MDT took the query out and `9b3bbc8bb8` of 13:36:05 took seven more. At HEAD the file is 48 lines and holds no query. I measured the original claim in a throwaway worktree at `d4db1f5107`, which is `841c0e961b^`, then removed the worktree.\n\nWHAT WAS THERE AT `d4db1f5107`. Lines 18-23 of that test read `const query = trackingOn(DAY) as Record<string, unknown>` and then `expect(query["page-type"]).toBe("daily-tracking")`, with `DAY` being `"2026-09-01"` at line 10. One correction to the brief: the assertion does not run over a kebab fixture. It runs over `trackingOn(DAY)`, the query the readout composes at `upkeep-surplus.readout.code.ts:17-24`, which returns `{ "page-type": DAY, where: ..., keys: [...], limit: 1 }`. Fixtures are imported at line 2 but used only by four other tests, and `readout-asking.module.test-fixtures.ts` is nine lines whose `answering` and `refusing` hand back what the caller supplies without looking at the query, so they could not have failed on the spelling either. The shape is narrower and worse than a wrong fixture: the assertion reads a value straight back out of the object under test, so it holds for whatever spelling that object uses.\n\nTHE SUITE COUNT. `bun test readout-system` from the checkout root: at `d4db1f5107`, `300 pass, 0 fail, 604 expect() calls, Ran 300 tests across 24 files`. At `841c0e961b`, 295 pass. At `9b3bbc8bb8`, 255 pass. At HEAD `832fa11720` I ran it myself: `255 pass, 0 fail, 543 expect() calls, Ran 255 tests across 24 files`. The 300 is exact rather than rounded.\n\nTHE QUERY REFUSES, RUN FOUR WAYS. `asking` has no CLI subcommand, so it was called directly against the live index for 2026-07-20. Spelled as the readout wrote it, `page-type` plus kebab keys: `{"refused":"`undefined` names no page type the index holds"}`. With `pageTypeSlug: "daily-tracking"` and kebab keys: `{"refused":"`daily-tracking` names no page type the index holds"}`. With `pageTypeSlug: "wake-day"` and kebab keys: refused for `keys` naming `surplus-hours`, which `wake-day` declares under no spelling. With that slug and camel keys: `{"rows":[{"surplusHours":-5.5,"sleepHours":7.5,"spendHours":13}]}`. Three faults each masking the next, and only the fourth spelling answers.\n\nWHY EACH FAULT IS REAL. `pages/system-service/page-asking/page-asking.module.code.ts:45-53` declares `Query` with `pageTypeSlug` and no `page-type`; lines 229-231 interpolate `query.pageTypeSlug` into the refusal, which is why an absent field prints as `undefined`. No `*.page-type.ts` carries `slug: "daily-tracking"` — it is a text-property, at `alan/tracking/daily/wake-days/properties/daily-tracking.text-property.ts:8`. Key matching is exact both ways: `unkeyed` at 179-188 rejects any `where`, `sortBy` or `keys` name outside the declared set, and `meets` at 59-66 does a bare lookup with no normalisation.\n\nWHY NOTHING TYPED CAUGHT IT. `readout-system/readout-asking/readout-asking.module.code.ts:7` declares `Asking` as `(query: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>) => Promise<Answered>`, so every object satisfies it. The reader that does work, `alan/tracking/daily/day-reading/day-reading.module.code.ts:45-58`, names `pageTypeSlug` and camelises keys inbound at line 49 — and still ends its query literal with `as never` at line 50, the same seam untyped from the other side.\n\nWHAT IS STILL THERE AT HEAD. Seven readout code files compose a query keyed `page-type`: `attribute-charisma`, `five-hour-back`, `upkeep-capacity`, `upkeep-safety`, `weekly-back`, `weekly-ends`, `weekly-usage`. Three of the seven name `session-tracking`, and no `*.page-type.ts` carries that slug; the only other file in the tree spelling it is a finding page\'s prose. The other four name `claude-account`, which is a real page type at `agents/claude-accounts/claude-account.page-type.ts:56`, so those four are wrong only in the key. Four assertions certify the wrong key: `upkeep-capacity.readout.test.ts:11`, `attribute-charisma.readout.test.ts:27`, `upkeep-safety.readout.test.ts:55` and `:96`, each expecting `"session-tracking"`. Meanwhile four `.module.code.ts` files in the same package spell `pageTypeSlug` correctly — `readout-scale-reading:22`, `readout-none-left:27`, `readout-serving:76`, `readout-group-serving:58` and `:113` — so both dialects sit side by side in one workspace package.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT ESTABLISH. I did not run the seven surviving queries against the index, so I have not shown those refuse — only that they carry the same `page-type` key that made `upkeep-surplus` refuse. I did not judge whether the repairs in flight intend to reach them. I did not measure how many other test files in the repository assert a value straight back off the object under test.',
