diff --git a/domains/findings/pages/page-asking-reads-the-checkout-while-page-calling-fetches-from-the-same-package.finding.ts b/domains/findings/pages/page-asking-reads-the-checkout-while-page-calling-fetches-from-the-same-package.finding.ts
index e3c710f62df7f74cb55b291ed49afc1816fe0c53..a64a765f79d7ab82be1dac1230848242a4f338f9 100644
--- a/domains/findings/pages/page-asking-reads-the-checkout-while-page-calling-fetches-from-the-same-package.finding.ts
+++ b/domains/findings/pages/page-asking-reads-the-checkout-while-page-calling-fetches-from-the-same-package.finding.ts
@@ -8,5 +8,5 @@ export const pageAskingReadsTheCheckoutWhilePageCallingFetchesFromTheSamePackage
   claim:
     "`@akasha/pages-service` holds both roads constraint 19 parts. `./asking` is the query engine and reads this checkout in the calling process; `./calling` is the HTTP client to the pod. So an import from that package is no evidence either way, and the package name is the trap rather than the guide: a lane reading the name will repair a compliant import, and a fetching export added to the package later makes every importer a violator with nothing to say so. Read the closure, not the name.",
   evidence:
-    'Measured 2026-09-03 while repointing `readouts/inbox-reading.ts`, which is on-workstation code and now imports `asking` from `@akasha/pages-service/asking` at line 39.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT WAS SEEDED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED. Each export was bundled from its own entry with `bun build --target=bun` and the bundle searched for `fetch(`, `new Request(`, `http://` and `https://`. The control, `page-calling`, is 4,198 bytes and answers 2, among them the origin `http://page-store.page-store.svc.cluster.local:8787`. The subject, `page-asking`, is 35,416 bytes over its whole transitive closure and answers 0, with no `node:http` and no `undici`. A false negative and a true negative are the same string, so the control is what makes the zero mean anything.\n\nThe positive half was measured too: the `asking` closure carries 8 `readFileSync` and 2 `readdirSync` and names `".git/data/index"`, which is the checkout road constraint 19 requires rather than the one it forbids. `asking` at `page-asking.module.code.ts:216` takes a root and reads it; `page-calling.module.code.ts:16` names the pod origin.\n\nWHY IT WILL BE RE-DERIVED. `akasha-answers-page-placement-directly-so-the-two-unrepointable-modules-repoint` rules `composedFor` a constraint-19 road on the ground that it is "exported from the package `pages/system-service/package.json:2` names `@akasha/pages-service`, which is the very thing constraint 19 forbids on-workstation code to call". That reasoning is from the package name, and applied to `./asking` it gives the wrong answer. `an-on-workstation-watcher-lands-completions-through-the-page-store-service` reaches its verdict the other way, by following `@akasha/pages-query` to `store-reaching.module.code.ts:9` and reading the origin there, and it says outright that a one-folder grep is the wrong instrument. The closure is the instrument; the manifest name is not.\n\nTHE CALL TAKEN. The import stands and `readouts/package.json` declares the dependency rather than hiding the coupling behind a relative path, which is what `tools/lib/tracking/akasha-day.ts:33` does for `page-composing`. A hidden dependency reads as compliant to the eye while being invisible to every tool that reads manifests.\n\nNot measured: whether the eight other exports of that package fetch. Only `./asking` and `./calling` were bundled.',
+    'Measured 2026-09-03 while repointing `readouts/inbox-reading.ts`, which is on-workstation code and now imports `asking` from `@akasha/pages-service/asking` at line 39.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT WAS SEEDED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED. Each export was bundled from its own entry with `bun build --target=bun` and the bundle searched for `fetch(`, `new Request(`, `http://` and `https://`. The control, `page-calling`, is 4,198 bytes and answers 2, among them the origin `http://page-store.page-store.svc.cluster.local:8787`. The subject, `page-asking`, is 35,416 bytes over its whole transitive closure and answers 0, with no `node:http` and no `undici`. A false negative and a true negative are the same string, so the control is what makes the zero mean anything.\n\nThe positive half was measured too: the `asking` closure carries 8 `readFileSync` and 2 `readdirSync` and names `".git/data/index"`, which is the checkout road constraint 19 requires rather than the one it forbids. `asking` at `page-asking.module.code.ts:216` takes a root and reads it; `page-calling.module.code.ts:16` names the pod origin.\n\nWHY IT WILL BE RE-DERIVED. `akasha-answers-page-placement-directly-so-the-two-unrepointable-modules-repoint` rules `composedFor` a constraint-19 road on the ground that it is "exported from the package `pages/service/package.json:2` names `@akasha/pages-service`, which is the very thing constraint 19 forbids on-workstation code to call". That reasoning is from the package name, and applied to `./asking` it gives the wrong answer. `an-on-workstation-watcher-lands-completions-through-the-page-store-service` reaches its verdict the other way, by following `@akasha/pages-query` to `store-reaching.module.code.ts:9` and reading the origin there, and it says outright that a one-folder grep is the wrong instrument. The closure is the instrument; the manifest name is not.\n\nTHE CALL TAKEN. The import stands and `readouts/package.json` declares the dependency rather than hiding the coupling behind a relative path, which is what `tools/lib/tracking/akasha-day.ts:33` does for `page-composing`. A hidden dependency reads as compliant to the eye while being invisible to every tool that reads manifests.\n\nNot measured: whether the eight other exports of that package fetch. Only `./asking` and `./calling` were bundled.',
 } as const satisfies Finding
